cation of filial obligation. Its role as an answer in Lear’s public test does not establish a separate residual force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to declare how much they love him, linking their answers to their shares. After Cordelia first answers “Nothing” and Lear commands her to speak again, she gives the focal restrained avowal rather than matching her sisters’ expansive declarations.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond and presents her love, obedience, and honour as duties returned to Lear. She does not make a new future promise in the focal expression. Lear does not accept the answer as adequate; he asks her to amend it and warns that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the passage as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear; the supplied source also identifies King Lear in its heading.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly presents Cordelia as loving Lear, including her aside, but it cannot independently establish private psychology beyond the literary representation. It also does not settle the complete history or eventual fulfilment of the invoked filial obligations.\"}"
